1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,620
Imagine holding half a billion dollars…

2
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:04,120
in your hand.

3
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:05,460
Not in a bank.

4
00:00:05,540 --> 00:00:07,840
In a tiny steel USB drive.

5
00:00:07,961 --> 00:00:09,421
There's just one problem.

6
00:00:09,441 --> 00:00:10,301
It's locked.

7
00:00:10,521 --> 00:00:12,181
And you've forgotten the password.

8
00:00:12,457 --> 00:00:13,857
You get ten tries.

9
00:00:13,957 --> 00:00:15,357
You've already used eight.

10
00:00:15,604 --> 00:00:16,384
Two left.

11
00:00:16,404 --> 00:00:17,084
Get them wrong…

12
00:00:17,104 --> 00:00:18,404
and the money is gone.

13
00:00:18,504 --> 00:00:19,124
Forever.

14
00:00:19,381 --> 00:00:19,921
Go on.

15
00:00:19,941 --> 00:00:20,861
Try.

16
00:00:21,304 --> 00:00:23,144
This isn't a thought experiment.

17
00:00:23,264 --> 00:00:24,664
It's happening right now,

18
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:26,193
to a real person.

19
00:00:26,213 --> 00:00:28,853
And when hackers offered to open it for him…

20
00:00:28,873 --> 00:00:29,913
he said no.

21
00:00:30,204 --> 00:00:33,884
To understand why, we have to go back to 2011.

22
00:00:36,223 --> 00:00:38,323
Stefan Thomas is a programmer.

23
00:00:38,783 --> 00:00:41,403
And in 2011, someone asked him to make a

24
00:00:41,423 --> 00:00:44,283
short animated video about a strange new

25
00:00:44,303 --> 00:00:45,803
thing called Bitcoin.

26
00:00:46,062 --> 00:00:47,082
His payment?

27
00:00:47,162 --> 00:00:48,982
Seven thousand and two bitcoin.

28
00:00:49,392 --> 00:00:52,232
Back then, that was basically pocket money.

29
00:00:52,442 --> 00:00:54,982
So he stored the keys somewhere very safe:

30
00:00:55,122 --> 00:00:56,382
an IronKey.

31
00:00:56,423 --> 00:00:59,823
An IronKey isn't a normal USB stick.

32
00:01:00,023 --> 00:01:02,203
It's built like a tiny bank vault.

33
00:01:02,443 --> 00:01:04,683
And it has one rule it never breaks.

34
00:01:05,004 --> 00:01:06,524
Ten wrong passwords…

35
00:01:06,544 --> 00:01:08,304
and it destroys the key inside.

36
00:01:08,844 --> 00:01:09,584
No reset.

37
00:01:09,804 --> 00:01:10,764
No recovery.

38
00:01:10,844 --> 00:01:12,744
No 'forgot password' button.

39
00:01:13,079 --> 00:01:16,199
So Stefan wrote his password on a piece of paper.

40
00:01:16,515 --> 00:01:18,535
You can probably guess what happened to

41
00:01:18,555 --> 00:01:19,335
the paper.

42
00:01:19,596 --> 00:01:20,796
He lost it.

43
00:01:21,752 --> 00:01:23,392
For a while, it didn't matter.

44
00:01:23,412 --> 00:01:24,772
Bitcoin was cheap.

45
00:01:24,845 --> 00:01:26,765
Then the price started to climb.

46
00:01:26,905 --> 00:01:28,145
A hundred dollars.

47
00:01:28,165 --> 00:01:28,985
A thousand.

48
00:01:29,005 --> 00:01:30,065
Ten thousand.

49
00:01:30,144 --> 00:01:31,704
And every time it went up,

50
00:01:31,724 --> 00:01:33,684
that little drive got heavier.

51
00:01:33,865 --> 00:01:35,625
So Stefan started guessing.

52
00:01:35,925 --> 00:01:37,885
The passwords he always used.

53
00:01:37,925 --> 00:01:38,825
Old favourites.

54
00:01:38,985 --> 00:01:39,865
Little variations.

55
00:01:40,375 --> 00:01:40,875
Wrong.

56
00:01:40,895 --> 00:01:41,295
Wrong.

57
00:01:41,315 --> 00:01:41,615
Three…

58
00:01:41,615 --> 00:01:41,915
four…

59
00:01:41,915 --> 00:01:42,275
five…

60
00:01:42,295 --> 00:01:42,835
six…

61
00:01:42,855 --> 00:01:43,243
seven…

62
00:01:43,263 --> 00:01:44,063
Eight.

63
00:01:44,876 --> 00:01:45,856
Two left.

64
00:01:46,636 --> 00:01:48,336
He told the New York Times:

65
00:01:48,756 --> 00:01:51,416
“I would just lay in bed and think about it.

66
00:01:51,496 --> 00:01:53,436
Then I would go to the computer with some

67
00:01:53,456 --> 00:01:55,396
new strategy, and it wouldn't work,

68
00:01:55,416 --> 00:01:57,716
and I would be desperate again.”

69
00:01:58,172 --> 00:01:59,372
Think about that.

70
00:01:59,572 --> 00:02:02,612
A fortune within reach, every single night.

71
00:02:02,692 --> 00:02:04,732
And two chances to lose it forever.

72
00:02:05,174 --> 00:02:06,454
In 2021,

73
00:02:06,614 --> 00:02:08,774
it was worth about two hundred and twenty

74
00:02:08,794 --> 00:02:10,254
million dollars.

75
00:02:10,514 --> 00:02:13,594
At bitcoin's peak, in October 2025,

76
00:02:13,874 --> 00:02:15,874
close to nine hundred million.

77
00:02:16,224 --> 00:02:16,744
So…

78
00:02:16,764 --> 00:02:18,384
why not just break it open?

79
00:02:18,740 --> 00:02:20,660
Because the IronKey remembers.

80
00:02:21,075 --> 00:02:23,155
Every wrong guess is counted inside a

81
00:02:23,175 --> 00:02:24,215
sealed chip.

82
00:02:24,475 --> 00:02:26,695
Unplug it, leave it in a drawer for a

83
00:02:26,715 --> 00:02:28,095
year, plug it back in…

84
00:02:28,115 --> 00:02:29,255
still eight.

85
00:02:29,571 --> 00:02:31,431
Try to drill into it, and you hit hard

86
00:02:31,451 --> 00:02:34,631
epoxy, built to stop exactly that.

87
00:02:34,651 --> 00:02:36,451
Even Kingston, the company that makes

88
00:02:36,471 --> 00:02:38,951
IronKeys today, said it couldn't help.

89
00:02:39,191 --> 00:02:40,031
No backdoor.

90
00:02:40,251 --> 00:02:41,511
No master key.

91
00:02:41,859 --> 00:02:43,059
So it's impossible.

92
00:02:43,299 --> 00:02:43,979
Right?

93
00:02:44,297 --> 00:02:44,837
Stick around.

94
00:02:44,857 --> 00:02:46,317
And subscribe…

95
00:02:46,337 --> 00:02:47,857
I'm very good at waiting.

96
00:02:48,251 --> 00:02:49,451
Here's the thing.

97
00:02:49,711 --> 00:02:50,951
Someone has already pulled off something

98
00:02:50,971 --> 00:02:52,091
like this.

99
00:02:52,169 --> 00:02:54,909
Meet Joe Grand, a hardware hacker.

100
00:02:55,229 --> 00:02:56,309
In 2021,

101
00:02:56,469 --> 00:02:58,829
a man named Dan Reich came to him with

102
00:02:58,849 --> 00:03:01,289
about two million dollars in crypto,

103
00:03:01,429 --> 00:03:04,189
locked in a little wallet called a Trezor.

104
00:03:04,209 --> 00:03:05,689
He'd forgotten the PIN.

105
00:03:05,762 --> 00:03:07,842
Twelve wrong guesses already.

106
00:03:08,062 --> 00:03:10,382
At sixteen, the wallet wipes itself.

107
00:03:10,602 --> 00:03:11,862
Four tries left.

108
00:03:12,010 --> 00:03:13,490
So Joe didn't guess.

109
00:03:13,550 --> 00:03:14,950
He went after the chip.

110
00:03:15,054 --> 00:03:16,634
During a firmware update,

111
00:03:16,654 --> 00:03:19,014
the wallet copies its PIN into memory.

112
00:03:19,354 --> 00:03:20,834
That memory is locked.

113
00:03:20,926 --> 00:03:22,926
But Joe hit the chip with a tiny,

114
00:03:22,946 --> 00:03:25,666
perfectly timed glitch in its power…

115
00:03:25,686 --> 00:03:28,166
and for a split second, the lock blinked.

116
00:03:28,554 --> 00:03:30,634
It took him twelve weeks of failures.

117
00:03:30,989 --> 00:03:33,309
And then, the PIN appeared on his screen.

118
00:03:33,594 --> 00:03:34,794
Two million dollars.

119
00:03:34,814 --> 00:03:35,454
Back.

120
00:03:35,789 --> 00:03:38,609
So could the same trick work on Stefan's drive?

121
00:03:38,732 --> 00:03:39,772
Not quite.

122
00:03:40,032 --> 00:03:42,532
That flaw was fixed back in 2017.

123
00:03:42,952 --> 00:03:45,512
And the IronKey is a much tougher machine.

124
00:03:45,913 --> 00:03:49,193
But in 2023, a security company called

125
00:03:49,213 --> 00:03:51,905
Unciphered said it had found a way in.

126
00:03:51,925 --> 00:03:53,265
Working with Wired,

127
00:03:53,445 --> 00:03:56,005
they took an IronKey of the same model,

128
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:58,305
carved out its chip, dissolved the

129
00:03:58,325 --> 00:04:00,347
protective epoxy with acid…

130
00:04:00,367 --> 00:04:02,027
…and ran about two hundred trillion

131
00:04:02,047 --> 00:04:03,547
password guesses.

132
00:04:04,518 --> 00:04:05,538
It opened.

133
00:04:06,249 --> 00:04:08,569
So they called Stefan with an offer: let

134
00:04:08,589 --> 00:04:10,289
us unlock your drive.

135
00:04:10,369 --> 00:04:10,709
And Stefan…

136
00:04:10,729 --> 00:04:11,929
said no.

137
00:04:12,832 --> 00:04:16,092
Why would anyone say no to half a billion dollars?

138
00:04:16,339 --> 00:04:17,839
According to reports,

139
00:04:17,959 --> 00:04:19,899
he had already hired another team,

140
00:04:19,919 --> 00:04:22,359
and promised them a share if they succeeded.

141
00:04:22,570 --> 00:04:24,890
So right now, somewhere, someone may be

142
00:04:24,910 --> 00:04:26,710
trying to open that drive.

143
00:04:26,987 --> 00:04:29,127
And as of today, there's no public

144
00:04:29,147 --> 00:04:31,467
confirmation that anyone has.

145
00:04:32,034 --> 00:04:33,614
And Stefan isn't alone.

146
00:04:33,752 --> 00:04:36,432
In Wales, a man named James Howells says

147
00:04:36,452 --> 00:04:38,732
he threw away a hard drive with around

148
00:04:38,752 --> 00:04:41,052
eight thousand bitcoin on it,

149
00:04:41,072 --> 00:04:42,492
back in 2013.

150
00:04:42,852 --> 00:04:44,652
It's buried in a landfill.

151
00:04:44,952 --> 00:04:47,652
He spent years in court trying to dig it up.

152
00:04:47,672 --> 00:04:49,812
In 2025, he lost.

153
00:04:50,229 --> 00:04:52,389
One estimate, from the blockchain firm

154
00:04:52,409 --> 00:04:53,629
Chainalysis,

155
00:04:53,769 --> 00:04:56,069
suggested that up to one in five bitcoins

156
00:04:56,089 --> 00:04:58,029
may be lost forever.

157
00:04:58,384 --> 00:05:00,544
So how do you make sure this never happens

158
00:05:00,564 --> 00:05:01,392
to you?

159
00:05:01,412 --> 00:05:03,172
Use a password manager,

160
00:05:03,332 --> 00:05:05,772
so your memory isn't the only backup.

161
00:05:05,803 --> 00:05:07,903
Write your recovery codes down,

162
00:05:07,923 --> 00:05:10,503
and keep copies in two safe places.

163
00:05:10,803 --> 00:05:12,222
Not one piece of paper.

164
00:05:12,242 --> 00:05:14,213
Test your backup before you need it.

165
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:16,033
And make sure someone you trust knows

166
00:05:16,053 --> 00:05:17,533
where to find it.

167
00:05:17,637 --> 00:05:19,977
Stefan called it a painful memory,

168
00:05:19,997 --> 00:05:21,997
and said he hopes others can learn from

169
00:05:22,017 --> 00:05:23,037
his mistakes.

170
00:05:23,437 --> 00:05:26,257
He said he was depressed for a few weeks…

171
00:05:26,277 --> 00:05:27,837
then decided to look forward,

172
00:05:27,857 --> 00:05:29,137
and keep building.

173
00:05:29,616 --> 00:05:31,176
So here's my question.

174
00:05:31,356 --> 00:05:32,136
Two guesses.

175
00:05:32,456 --> 00:05:34,436
More than half a billion dollars.

176
00:05:34,556 --> 00:05:35,976
Would you try them yourself…

177
00:05:35,996 --> 00:05:37,996
or hand the drive to a hacker?

178
00:05:38,156 --> 00:05:39,456
Tell me in the comments.

179
00:05:39,677 --> 00:05:41,777
And subscribe, because next time,

180
00:05:41,797 --> 00:05:43,657
we're digging into that landfill.

181
00:05:44,028 --> 00:05:45,948
And I thought my sentence was bad.

182
00:05:46,055 --> 00:05:47,055
Two guesses.

183
00:05:47,075 --> 00:05:48,195
I'll be waiting.
